.TH "cmconvert" 1 "@INFERNAL_DATE@" "Infernal @INFERNAL_VERSION@" "Infernal Manual"

.SH NAME
cmconvert - convert Infernal covariance model files

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmconvert
.I [options]
.I <cmfile>

.SH DESCRIPTION

.PP
The
.B cmconvert 
utility converts an input covariance model file to different Infernal
formats.

.PP
By default, the input CM file can be any CM file created by Infernal
version 1.0 or later; the output CM file is a current Infernal
format. Files from versions older than version 1.0 cannot be
converted.

.PP
CM files written by Infernal versions before 1.2 store a separately
built filter HMM after each CM. Infernal 1.2 reads them and uses that
stored filter HMM, and by default
.B cmconvert
writes such a CM in an older format again (INFERNAL1/b, or
INFERNAL1/d for a CM with pseudoknot annotation), keeping its filter
HMM. Use
.B --upgrade
to convert such CMs to the current format (INFERNAL1/e), which stores
no filter HMM.

.PP
.I <cmfile> 
may be '-' (dash), which
means reading this input from
.I stdin
rather than a file.  

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B -h
Help: print a brief reminder of command line usage and available
options, and exit.

.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version information: print the program name and Infernal version, and exit.

.TP 
.B -a
Output profiles in ASCII text format. This is the default.

.TP 
.B -b
Output profiles in binary format. 

.TP 
.B -1
Output in legacy Infernal1 (Infernal v1.0 through v1.0.2) ASCII text
format. Due to important changes between version v1.0.2 and v1.1, any
E-value statistic parameters calculated by
.B cmcalibrate
in 
.B <cmfile>
will not be written to the converted output file. 

.TP 
.B --mlhmm
Do not output a CM file. Instead, output one maximum likelihood p7 HMM
built from each CM in 
.B <cmfile>
in HMMER3 ASCII text format. The HMM will have been constructed to be
as similar as possible to the CM, without modeling secondary
structure. This option could be useful for comparative studies of
Infernal and HMMER3.

.TP
.B --fhmm
Do not output a CM file. Instead, output the filter p7 HMM
for each CM in
.B <cmfile>
in HMMER3 ASCII text format. For a CM in the current format, this is
the HMM derived from the CM when it is read (its maximum likelihood p7
HMM); for a CM in an older format, it is the filter HMM stored in the
file.

.TP
.B --cp9ascm
Do not output the original CM. Instead, output an unstructured CM
(a linear chain of MATL nodes, with no base pairs) built from each
CM's ML CP9 HMM
.B ( cm->cp9 ),
in Infernal ASCII text format. Unlike
.B --mlhmm
and
.B --fhmm ,
which output the p7/HMMER3-format HMMs associated with the CM, this
option outputs the CM Plan 9 HMM itself, represented as a CM, so
that it can be aligned or searched against with
.B cmalign
or
.B cmsearch
like any other (unstructured) CM. Only CMs configured in global mode
are currently supported (this is the default state of a freshly-read
CM file).

.TP
.BI --outfmt " <s>"
With
.B -a
or
.B -b,
write the CMs in an older Infernal 1.1 format,
.I <s>
= 1/a, 1/b or 1/d, for use by older versions of Infernal. These
formats store a filter HMM after each CM; a CM in the current format
has none, so its filter HMM (its maximum likelihood p7 HMM) is stored.

.TP
.B --dropknots
Allow a conversion that loses consensus pseudoknot annotation. Only
the INFERNAL1/d and current (INFERNAL1/e) CM formats can
store a CM's consensus pseudoknot annotation. Converting such a CM to
an older format (with
.B -1,
or with
.B --outfmt 1/a
or
.B --outfmt 1/b )
is an error unless
.B --dropknots
is used, in which case the annotation is silently left out of the
output. CMs without pseudoknot annotation are unaffected.

.TP
.B --upgrade
Convert CMs in older formats (INFERNAL1/a, /b, /d, and Infernal v1.0)
to the current format (INFERNAL1/e), the format
.B cmbuild
writes. The filter HMM stored after each CM is discarded; the two
profile HMMs that Infernal 1.2 derives from a CM, the filter HMM and
the HMM-only search HMM (for
.B cmsearch
and
.BR "cmscan --hmmonly" ),
are derived from the CM and calibrated for E-values as
.B cmbuild
does, so the output CM is the one that
.B cmbuild
would have written, without needing the CM's training alignment.
The CM itself and its own E-value parameters (from
.BR cmcalibrate )
are unchanged. Per-CM filter P-value cutoffs (from
.BR "cmbuild --fil-pcut" )
are dropped, because they belonged to the old filter HMM.
P7 band pads are kept, unless
.B --p7pad
recomputes them for the derived filter HMM.
CMs already in the current format are written unchanged.

.IP
How the HMM-only search HMM is made, and the calibration settings,
depend on how the CM was built, which
.B --upgrade
reads from the
.B cmbuild
command on the CM's COM line: the transition prior
.RB ( --p56
and
.B --v1p0
use the version 1.0 prior, as do Infernal v1.0 files; otherwise the
default prior), whether a CM with no basepairs is parameterized like
a profile HMM, and
.B cmbuild's
filter HMM calibration options. If the CM was built with
.B cmbuild --prior <f>,
give the same prior file with
.B --prior <f>;
otherwise its HMM-only search HMM is its filter HMM, and a note is
printed to stderr. Calibration takes about as long as it does in
.B cmbuild:
seconds for most CMs, and minutes for CMs of tens of thousands of
positions or more.

.TP
.BI --prior " <f>"
With
.B --upgrade,
the CMs were built with
.B cmbuild --prior <f>.
The prior file's transition priors determine the HMM-only search HMM.

.TP
.BI --cpu " <n>"
With
.B --upgrade,
the number of parallel worker threads for calibrating the filter HMMs.

.SH SEE ALSO

See 
.B infernal(1)
for a master man page with a list of all the individual man pages
for programs in the Infernal package.

.PP
For complete documentation, see the user guide that came with your
Infernal distribution (Userguide.pdf); or see the Infernal web page
(@INFERNAL_URL@).


